## Injecting code

the place where the code is injected is called context

```elixir
defmodule Mod do
  defmacro definfo do
    IO.puts "In macros context (#{__MODULE__})"
    quote do
      IO.puts "In callers contex (#{__MODULE__})"
      def friendly_info do
        IO.puts """
        My name is #{__MODULE__}
        My function are #{inspect __MODULE__.__info__(:functions)}
        """
      end   
    end
  end
end
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:module, Mod, <<70, 79, 82, 49, 0, 0, 12, ...>>, ...}
```

```elixir
defmodule MyModule do
  require Mod
  Mod.definfo 
  IO.inspect(Module.defines?(__MODULE__, {:friendly_info, 0}, :def))
  Mod.friendly_info # -> yields (UndefinedFunctionError) function MyModule.friendly_info/0 is undefined (function not available). Make sure the module name is correct and has been specified in full (or that an alias has been defined)
end

# outside works.
MyModule.friendly_info
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
In macros context (Elixir.Mod)
In callers contex (Elixir.MyModule)
true
```

