macro hygine, means how we access scope beetween, calllers, the cleanes way possible. but sometimes migth be an unnecesary evil

```elixir
ast = quote do
  if meaning_of_life == 42 do
    "its true"
  else
    "tbd"
  end
end
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:if, [context: Elixir, imports: [{2, Kernel}]],
 [
   {:==, [context: Elixir, imports: [{2, Kernel}]], [{:meaning_of_life, [], Elixir}, 42]},
   [do: "its true", else: "tbd"]
 ]}
```

```elixir
Code.eval_quoted(ast)
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
error: undefined variable "meaning_of_life" (context Elixir)
└─ nofile:1

```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
** (CompileError) cannot compile cell (errors have been logged)
```

the previous error is cause elixir is not able to access the outside scope.

```elixir
ast = quote do
  if var!(meaning_of_life) == 42 do
    "true"
  else
    "tbd"
  end
end
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:if, [context: Elixir, imports: [{2, Kernel}]],
 [
   {:==, [context: Elixir, imports: [{2, Kernel}]],
    [
      {:var!, [context: Elixir, imports: [{1, Kernel}, {2, Kernel}]],
       [{:meaning_of_life, [], Elixir}]},
      42
    ]},
   [do: "true", else: "tbd"]
 ]}
```

```elixir
Code.eval_quoted(ast, meaning_of_life: 42)
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{"true", [meaning_of_life: 42]}
```

```elixir
Code.eval_quoted(ast, meaning_of_life: 100)
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{"tbd", [meaning_of_life: 100]}
```

another example for macro higiene

```elixir
  defmodule Setter do
    defmacro bind_name(string) do
      quote do
        name =  unquote(string)
      end
    end
  end
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:module, Setter, <<70, 79, 82, 49, 0, 0, 7, ...>>, ...}
```

```elixir
require Setter
name = "Alejandro"

```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
"Alejandro"
```

```elixir
Setter.bind_name("Martino")
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
"Martino"
```

```elixir
name
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
"Alejandro"
```

```elixir
  defmodule SetterNotHygiene do
    defmacro bind_name(string) do
      quote do
        # here it access the caller context, by default
        # it tries to protect the context unless specified
        var!(name_not_h) =  unquote(string)
      end
    end
  end
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:module, SetterNotHygiene, <<70, 79, 82, 49, 0, 0, 7, ...>>, ...}
```

```elixir
require SetterNotHygiene
name_not_h = "Alejandro"
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
"Alejandro"
```

```elixir
SetterNotHygiene.bind_name("Martino")
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
"Martino"
```

```elixir
name_not_h
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
"Martino"
```

we should avoid using this trick since it can make things obscure to the caller, this is called hygiene override, only when absolutely necessary. when working with macros it is important to be aware of where is the macro being called

